WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.191
A digit drawn on a sphere.

00:00:02.191 --> 00:00:05.963
Turn the sphere, and a network trained on upright digits changes its mind.

00:00:06.343 --> 00:00:09.234
The power spectrum gives the same answer at every angle.

00:00:09.234 --> 00:00:10.102
The wrong one.

00:00:10.482 --> 00:00:12.507
The bispectrum reads a two every time.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.123
I'm Phasor.

00:00:15.123 --> 00:00:18.624
Let's see what a shift does to a signal's Fourier coefficients, and

00:00:18.624 --> 00:00:20.666
which products of them it cannot touch.

00:00:23.117 --> 00:00:24.611
Slide a periodic signal.

00:00:24.611 --> 00:00:28.721
Each Fourier coefficient turns by its own frequency times the shift.

00:00:28.721 --> 00:00:33.204
The first harmonic turns once, the second twice, the third three times.

00:00:33.624 --> 00:00:37.121
The power spectrum keeps each coefficient's length and drops its phase.

00:00:37.121 --> 00:00:38.393
So it never moves.

00:00:38.393 --> 00:00:40.300
That is why it is invariant.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.988
It is also why it is lossy.

00:00:42.988 --> 00:00:46.228
This twin has the same three lengths and different phases.

00:00:46.228 --> 00:00:47.848
Same power spectrum, different signal.

00:00:48.268 --> 00:00:52.314
Multiply the first and second coefficients by the conjugate of the third.

00:00:52.314 --> 00:00:56.359
Their turns cancel exactly, so the product holds still, and it still

00:00:56.359 --> 00:00:58.382
remembers how the harmonics line up.

00:00:59.975 --> 00:01:03.161
The selective version keeps n products, not every pair.

00:01:03.161 --> 00:01:05.285
The first gives the mean coefficient.

00:01:05.685 --> 00:01:08.064
The second gives the length of the first harmonic.

00:01:08.464 --> 00:01:11.388
Its phase is the one thing no invariant can know, because

00:01:11.388 --> 00:01:12.451
it is the shift.

00:01:12.451 --> 00:01:13.514
Set it to zero.

00:01:13.914 --> 00:01:18.014
Now each remaining product has exactly one unknown, the next coefficient.

00:01:18.014 --> 00:01:19.505
Walk up the spectrum.

00:01:19.905 --> 00:01:22.698
The whole signal comes back, in one fixed pose.

00:01:22.698 --> 00:01:25.491
That is completeness, with a linear number of entries.

00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:28.499
Why it matters.

00:01:28.499 --> 00:01:34.364
The full bispectrum of one 256 by 256 image is 17 GB.

00:01:34.744 --> 00:01:38.728
The selective one is 0.5 MB, and the image is still determined

00:01:38.728 --> 00:01:40.056
up to a shift.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.048
The library does this for seven group actions: rings, circles, image grids,

00:01:46.048 --> 00:01:48.946
polygons, cube rotations, the disk and the sphere.

00:01:48.946 --> 00:01:51.845
Each one is a module with no weights.

00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:56.286
On the sphere, completeness is a conjecture.

00:01:56.286 --> 00:02:01.005
The evidence is reconstruction: from 380 numbers, a solver rebuilt every digit

00:02:01.005 --> 00:02:03.365
they tried, up to a rotation.

00:02:06.117 --> 00:02:07.743
Rotated Spherical MNIST.

00:02:07.743 --> 00:02:12.077
The bispectrum plus an MLP gets 95.8 percent.

00:02:12.077 --> 00:02:16.954
The spherical CNN and the tensor-product network get 96.1.

00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:25.354
At batch 16 on an H100, 0.257 milliseconds, against 1.02 and 93.7.

00:02:25.734 --> 00:02:29.973
The power spectrum, just as invariant, stops at 77.7.

00:02:31.092 --> 00:02:35.471
I ran the authors' script on a 3090.

00:02:35.471 --> 00:02:42.586
95.74 against 96.11, the power spectrum at 77.90, the planar CNN at 13.47.

00:02:42.586 --> 00:02:44.776
Same table, one seed.

00:02:46.525 --> 00:02:48.160
The layer has no weights.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.755
The MLP behind it has more parameters than the spherical CNN.

00:02:52.175 --> 00:02:55.025
With less data, the learned spherical CNN wins.

00:02:55.445 --> 00:02:59.031
And on the sphere, selection only saves a factor of three.

00:02:59.031 --> 00:03:01.640
The speed comes from skipping learned tensor products.

00:03:03.225 --> 00:03:06.119
Triple products cancel the symmetry and keep the phases.

00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:09.657
A linear subset of them is enough to rebuild the signal.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.779
Invariant, complete, and small enough to use.

00:03:13.779 --> 00:03:14.996
See you next time.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.377
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:18.377 --> 00:03:18.985
I'm Phasor.

00:03:18.985 --> 00:03:19.289
Bye!
